---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";

const csv = readFileSync("public/data/snow-emergency-jefferson-tows.csv", "utf8").trim();
const [headerLine, ...lines] = csv.split("\n");
const headers = headerLine.split(",");
const rows = lines.map((l) => l.split(","));
---

<Layout
    title="One Dataset, Many Interpretations | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Everyone gets the same never-before-seen dataset at the same moment. Build what you see in it, then share how differently we all read the same numbers."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: One Dataset, Many Interpretations
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, Sep 26 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CDT
                </p>
                <p class="flex items-start gap-2 mt-1">
                    <svg
                        class="w-5 h-5 mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-gray-400"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        fill="currentColor"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                        <path
                            fill-rule="evenodd"
                            d="M12 2c-3.866 0-7 3.07-7 6.857 0 4.34 4.686 9.86 6.412 11.74a.8.8 0 0 0 1.176 0C14.314 18.717 19 13.197 19 8.857 19 5.07 15.866 2 12 2Zm0 9.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0-5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0 5Z"
                            clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                    </svg>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Coffee Shop NE</span
                        ><br />2852 Johnson St NE · Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                One dataset. A room full of ways to see it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Nobody sees the dataset until the clock starts. Then everyone
                gets the same data at the same moment, with total freedom of
                tools: build whatever you see in it, share it in sixty
                seconds, and discover how differently the room read the same
                numbers.
            </p>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-2xl w-full rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
                    <img
                        src="/images/one-dataset-colorful-worksheet.png"
                        alt="A classic Macintosh spreadsheet titled Worksheet1 with every cell filled in a different bright color"
                        class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                    />
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                The dataset: every tow from the Jefferson snow emergency
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Minneapolis names its snow emergencies alphabetically. Jefferson
                was declared in late December 2025, and over its three days the
                city towed {rows.length} cars. Each row is one tow: the date,
                which day of the emergency it fell on, the street address, the
                neighborhood, community, ward and tow zone, and a latitude and
                longitude.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Source: <a
                    href="https://opendata.minneapolis.gov/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Minneapolis Open Data</a
                >, Snow Emergency Jefferson Tows 2025. Column names were tidied;
                the values are untouched. A few rows have a blank neighborhood
                and a ward and tow zone of 0. That is in the source too, and
                what you do with it is one of the interpretations.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-8">
                <a
                    href="/data/snow-emergency-jefferson-tows.csv"
                    download
                    class="inline-block bg-blue-600 text-white font-semibold px-5 py-3 rounded-lg hover:bg-blue-700 transition-colors"
                    >Download the CSV ({rows.length} rows)</a
                >
            </p>

            <div class="overflow-auto max-h-[36rem] border border-gray-200 rounded-lg mb-12">
                <table class="min-w-full text-sm text-left text-gray-700">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-900 sticky top-0">
                        <tr>
                            {headers.map((h) => (
                                <th class="px-3 py-2 font-semibold whitespace-nowrap">{h}</th>
                            ))}
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        {rows.map((r) => (
                            <tr class="border-t border-gray-100 odd:bg-white even:bg-gray-50">
                                {r.map((c) => (
                                    <td class="px-3 py-1.5 whitespace-nowrap">{c}</td>
                                ))}
                            </tr>
                        ))}
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                RSVP on <a
                    href="https://www.meetup.com/tcplot/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Meetup</a
                >.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
